Z||000000||Toughguy||12-29-2001||03:31 PM||||Hi,<BR>in a Fiero owners issue from 1994 there is an article on upgrading (performance wise)the TH125 tranny,written by Max Owens.He used a Transgo shift kit and another intermediate servo,with a small diameter of 1.246" instead of the original 1.662".He stated that some part numbers are from the `84,but it seams that FOCOA had shortened the text,so there are no # left,but the club sells all needed parts for $95.The shift kit is around $16,and I don´t think the other parts are around $80 worth.<BR>My question <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">id anyone accomplish this mod,does it really work much better than only the shift kit?<BR>Maybe Mark Owens is in the Forum or anybody know his e mail?<P>Greets,<BR>LUD ||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||12-29-2001||04:07 PM||||I haven't seen the article... can't say what he's trying to accomplish with the servo change. It's a hell of allot of work. Most of the tranny has to come apart.<P>The shift kit can be done easy enough.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Toughguy||12-29-2001||04:19 PM||||He tries to modifie the tranny in that way,that he uses a servo with a smaller inner diameter an so applying more pressure.you can do this through the oil pan opening,that means oil pan removal,and some other minor parts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||12-29-2001||06:00 PM||||I haven't seen the article so I may talking out of my ______ here.  If they switched to a smaller servo, then the goal would be for a quicker shift not more holding power.  That would make sense as re-routing oil flow for shift speed is marginal with smaller pumps so the next link in the chain would be to get a servo that will 'fill' faster.||||reg||1
